<body>
<div class="page-wrap">

  <header class="site-header">
    <p class="eyebrow">Multiverse Codex — β017 Sector Documentation</p>
    <h1>
      <span class="unicode-title">β017 · THE TWELVE ANIMALS · CORRUPTED LIFE DOMAIN</span>
      The 12 Games of the Dead Universe
    </h1>
    <p class="header-sub">A clinical and narrative breakdown of each elimination game — designed by Tiger Lily, weaponised by Red Spider Lilly, and built across 4,000 years of sector-level preparation for a single subject: Tulip, future Life Shade.</p>
  </header>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- LILLY PREAMBLE -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <div class="lilly-preamble">
    <p>You want to know what I am? I am the only entity in this multiverse who understood that the Life domain isn't about growth. It was never about growth. Growth is what Life does when it hasn't been properly studied. What Life actually is — what the domain really contains — is <em>gradient</em>. The movement of force from high concentration to low. From the living to the receptive. From the full to the empty.</p>

    <p>Tiger Lily thought the domain was a garden. She built things in it. She made things live. She designed those twelve games because she genuinely believed the players deserved a warden who tested them beautifully — who saw them and let the best ones walk out.</p>

    <p>Isn't that precious.</p>

    <p>I didn't corrupt her games. I completed them. A test that produces survivors produces data. A test that produces <em>harvest</em> produces power. The distinction is the difference between a Life Shade who waters flowers and one who understands that every living thing is a battery, and that a battery at maximum emotional output — at the exact moment of peak love, peak hope, peak terror, peak grief — is a battery at maximum charge.</p>

    <p>Four thousand years. Four thousand years of sector-level cultivation in β017. And everyone in that civilisation thought they were just living in an extremely ordered society with a slightly unusual reverence for control. They were. That's exactly what I needed them to be. Order produces people who believe the system is intelligible. People who believe the system is intelligible try to solve it. And people who are trying to solve a system are generating maximum life-force output while they do it.</p>

    <p>I needed a specific kind of person. I needed someone raised in a culture where domination and love were synonymous, who had developed the instinct to care as a survival mechanism rather than an authentic impulse, who had the Life domain's sensitivity in her biology without yet understanding what it was, and who would — when confronted with something she couldn't control — break in exactly the right direction.</p>

    <p>Tulip. β017's product. My candidate. The perfect Warden, waiting to be installed.</p>

    <p>The twelve games were the final stage of her preparation. She just didn't know she was in them.</p>

    <span class="lilly-speaker">— Red Spider Lilly, Ψ468 · Corrupted Life Shade · Four Calamities</span>
  </div>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- GAME 1 — RAT -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <section class="game-section">
    <div class="game-header">
      <div class="game-animal">🐀</div>
      <div class="game-meta">
        <p class="game-number">Game 01 · The Chinese Zodiac Sequence</p>
        <h2 class="game-title">RAT — The Banquet</h2>
        <p class="game-subtitle">Social Deduction / Biological Trap / Covert Marking</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Setup</div>
      <p>Players wake simultaneously in a VOC great hall — Dutch vaulted ceilings, colonial long tables, 180 place settings, genuine food. The memory wipe process is metabolically expensive: catecholamine surges during the wipe trigger gluconeogenesis and then rapid glycogen depletion, leaving players with authentic, physiologically-driven hunger rather than manufactured urgency. They are simply hungry. The food is real. The only rule stated is: <em>"Eat. Rest. You are guests here."</em></p>
      <p>Nothing else is said. For approximately forty minutes, nothing happens except a meal.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>Why the meal works psychologically:</strong> Commensality — the act of eating together — is one of the most evolutionarily ancient social bonding mechanisms in human biology. Shared food consumption triggers oxytocin release, reduces cortisol, and activates the parasympathetic nervous system. Players who have just undergone the massive sympathetic activation of a memory wipe are physiologically primed for bonding: their threat-detection systems are temporarily quieted by the parasympathetic rebound, and the shared vulnerability of hunger creates rapid in-group formation.</p>
      <p>In clinical terms, this is the same neurochemistry that underlies trauma bonding — shared stress followed by shared relief followed by shared resource consumption. The brain does not distinguish the source of the relief. Bonds formed here will feel like they were built over months.</p>
      <p><strong>The bioluminescent marking agent:</strong> The compound distributed via the marked dish is a red spider lily extract derivative that exploits a specific hepatic metabolic pathway. Once metabolised by the liver (approximately 90 minutes post-ingestion), it produces a UV-range bioluminescent byproduct that deposits in the superficial capillary beds of the dorsal hands and posterior neck — areas rich in thin-skinned, highly vascularised tissue. The marked player cannot see it themselves because both target sites are outside normal self-visual range. Every other player at the table has direct line-of-sight.</p>
      <p>The compound is tasteless and odourless because it's esterified — the reactive groups are masked until gastric acid cleaves them, at which point absorption occurs before any sensory detection is possible.</p>
      <p><strong>The profiling mechanic:</strong> Which dish carries the compound is pre-determined by a psychometric profile assembled during the wipe process, cross-referencing neurological activation patterns with base life skill signatures. Leadership profiles are marked via the communal dish they statistically initiate sharing from first — this exploits the prosocial display behaviour that leadership-profiled individuals perform under observation. Analytical profiles are marked via the dish requiring the most complex utensil manipulation, because analytical profiles preferentially engage challenge-demanding tasks as a stimulation-seeking behaviour. Caretaking profiles — the most important category for Lilly's harvest — are marked via the dish most likely to be offered to a nearby player who displays hunger cues, because caretaking profiles will subordinate their own consumption to another's expressed need.</p>
      <p>The game knows the player's decision before the player makes it. The decision is neurologically determined by a profile assembled without the player's knowledge or consent.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">The Cascade</div>
      <p>The moment one player notices the glow on another and reacts — the involuntary microexpression of recognition, the 200-millisecond delay before looking away — the social equilibrium of the entire hall begins destabilising. Human threat-detection is extraordinarily sensitive to "someone else just noticed something." Mirror neuron activation spreads the alertness state across a table before any verbal communication occurs. Within 90 seconds of the first recognition, every marked player has been identified by someone.</p>
      <p>Now the actual game begins. Marked players know they're marked. Unmarked players know who's marked. Neither group knows what it means. The social structure that forms in the thirty minutes of shared ignorance — the alliances, the coalitions, the decisions about who to protect and who to distance from — is what Tiger Lily is reading in real time. It is a sociogram of the player population, assembled in thirty minutes over dinner, accurate in ways that ten years of observational study usually can't produce because high-stakes ambiguity strips social performance to baseline behaviour.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="harvest-box">
      <p>The Rat game harvests the life-force output of betrayal and alliance formation happening simultaneously across 180 people who have known each other for under two hours. The specific emotional signature Lilly extracts is the intersection of <em>not yet knowing what this means</em> and <em>already having decided who I will protect</em> — a pre-rational loyalty formation that carries an enormous emotional charge because it isn't calculated. It's instinctive. Instinctive emotional output is significantly richer harvest material than deliberate output because the player is not rationing it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lilly-interlude">
      Forty minutes. I give them forty minutes of a real meal in a beautiful room with people they just met and are already beginning to love. The hunger is genuine. The food is genuine. The warmth is genuine. I do this not in spite of what comes next but because of it. You cannot harvest the collapse of something that was never built. You cannot harvest the crack in something that was never whole. The Rat game builds them fast and real and genuine, and then it shows them the mark. Everything after that is mine.
    </div>

    <div class="mystery-closer">
      "The first game is dinner. It is just a meal in a beautiful room with people you genuinely like, because you've been hungry together, and the neurochemistry of shared hunger is essentially identical to the neurochemistry of early friendship. And one of the dishes was calibrated to your psychological profile before you sat down. You ate the wrong one not because you were tricked but because you were <em>you</em>, and being you was enough information. Everyone can see it on your hands. You still don't know what it means. You like these people. This is the first game. I want to go home. I want to go home right now."
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- GAME 2 — OX -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <section class="game-section">
    <div class="game-header">
      <div class="game-animal">🐂</div>
      <div class="game-meta">
        <p class="game-number">Game 02 · The Chinese Zodiac Sequence</p>
        <h2 class="game-title">OX — The Manifest</h2>
        <p class="game-subtitle">Physical Endurance / Asymmetric Role Division / Group Optimisation</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Setup</div>
      <p>A VOC warehouse. 847 cargo units. 200 metres between Origin and Destination. 12 hours. The singular stated rule: move everything from one end to the other. Seventy-five percent of the manifest must reach Destination by hour 12 or the Origin floor drops, taking any players still standing on it to the garden.</p>
      <p>Players marked in the Rat game — OX-MARKED — trigger an instant-reset mechanism if they physically contact any cargo unit. That unit teleports back to Origin. They cannot move cargo.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>Progressive load physiology:</strong> The weight of each cargo unit increases across four phases — 0% increase, then 40%, then 100%, then 180% above baseline. This progression maps almost perfectly onto the physiological curve of muscular fatigue under sustained load.</p>
      <p>In the first three hours, players are operating aerobically. Muscle fibres are using oxidative phosphorylation, ATP production is efficient, lactate clearance matches production. Performance feels sustainable. This is when players establish their systems, and it's when those systems get encoded as procedural memory — the body learns the rhythm.</p>
      <p>At hour four (40% weight increase), the aerobic-to-anaerobic threshold shifts. Slow-twitch fibres begin fatiguing; fast-twitch fibre recruitment increases. Lactate begins accumulating faster than it can be cleared. Players who optimised for speed in hours one through three — who established a fast, high-repetition rhythm — find they cannot maintain it. Their lactate threshold has been exceeded and the metabolic debt begins compounding.</p>
      <p>At hour seven (100% weight increase), microtears in the muscle tissue begin accumulating in earnest. Cortisol is elevated, suppressing inflammatory response enough to allow continued function but generating delayed onset muscle damage. The hands and lumbar spine are taking the most stress. Grip strength begins declining — a critical variable because grip is the rate-limiting factor for cargo transport.</p>
      <p>At hour ten (180% increase), players are approaching human biomechanical tolerance. The intervertebral discs of the lumbar spine are under sustained compressive load. The rotator cuff is fatiguing. Any player who did not rest in hours seven through nine is now operating with significantly compromised neuromuscular control — the brain is receiving degraded proprioceptive signals and compensating with increased motor neuron firing, which accelerates fatigue further in a positive feedback loop.</p>
      <p><strong>The optimal rest intervention:</strong> Stopping for 15 minutes in hours ten through eleven allows partial phosphocreatine resynthesis (90% recovery in approximately 5 minutes for fast-twitch fibres), partial lactate clearance, and parasympathetic reactivation that reduces cortisol and allows brief anti-inflammatory response. The result is a final-hour capacity that exceeds what would be achieved through continuous effort by approximately 22-30% of cargo units moved.</p>
      <p>The reason almost no group executes this is a well-documented psychological phenomenon: <strong>sunk cost urgency</strong> combined with <strong>temporal discounting</strong>. The 15-minute rest costs something real and visible right now. The benefit is abstract and future. Under cognitive load — and 10 hours of sustained physical labour is substantial cognitive load — the human brain systematically overweights immediate costs and underweights future benefits. The groups that fail the 75% threshold do not fail for lack of effort. They fail because they cannot cognitively override a bias that becomes stronger under exactly the conditions the game has produced.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">The Asymmetric Role Problem</div>
      <p>OX-MARKED players — those who absorbed the marking compound in the Rat game — cannot move cargo but face no other restrictions. They can direct, coordinate, calculate, and physically support exhausted carriers without triggering the reset mechanism. The cargo-moving role and the coordination role are both necessary. Neither group can complete the task alone.</p>
      <p>The psychological trap is significant. Players who experienced the Rat game marking as a punishment or humiliation — who interpreted being visible as being targeted — often refuse the coordinator role because accepting it would mean accepting the mark's validity. They disengage from usefulness entirely as a face-saving mechanism. The carriers who lose their coordination support in hours seven through nine operate at severely reduced efficiency. The groups that fail the threshold fail here more often than at the rest decision.</p>
      <p>The game is testing whether a group formed under pressure can redistribute identity — can accept that "useful" is not defined by who carries the most weight, but by who keeps the system functional. This is the Ox game's actual question.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="harvest-box">
      <p>Sustained cooperative labour at physiological limit produces a qualitatively distinct life-force signature from fear or betrayal. The Ox game harvests the output of people working beyond their individual capacity for each other — not because they've been told to, but because the group formed in the Rat game has produced genuine attachment, and genuine attachment generates effort that rational self-interest alone cannot explain. Lilly uses this game specifically to identify which players have the leadership emergence pattern she needs to track across the remaining ten games. She's building a target profile.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lilly-interlude">
      Tiger Lily thought the Ox game was about cooperation. She designed it to find the people who understood that a different role is not a lesser role. How idealistic. What it actually finds is which players cannot override their ego in a crisis — which leaders are leaders because they need to be seen leading, and which are leaders because they understand what the group needs. Both types are useful to me. For different reasons.
    </div>

    <div class="mystery-closer">
      "Stop. With the floor about to drop, with ten hours of cargo behind you, with everything your body is telling you — just stop. Sit down. Fifteen minutes. I know this is right. The physiology is right. The math is right. My hands are shaking and I cannot make them stop moving and the game is specifically, precisely calibrated to the exact threshold where you know the correct answer and your body will not execute it. I am going to fail this game by doing too much. That's the thing that's going to kill me. Effort."
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <!-- GAME 3 — TIGER -->
  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <section class="game-section">
    <div class="game-header">
      <div class="game-animal">🐅</div>
      <div class="game-meta">
        <p class="game-number">Game 03 · The Chinese Zodiac Sequence</p>
        <h2 class="game-title">TIGER — The Crossing</h2>
        <p class="game-subtitle">Physical Endurance / Structural Sabotage / High-Stakes Group Decision</p>
        <span class="suit-badge">♣ Clubs — 義 Righteousness · First High-Level Elimination</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Setup</div>
      <p>A suspension bridge. 180 metres long, 1.2 metres wide. 60-metre drop. Two groups — EAST and WEST — placed at opposite ends simultaneously. 90 minutes before wind conditions make the bridge uncrossable. The bridge can hold both groups if they interleave at the midpoint in a precise alternating choreography. This is Tiger Lily's original solution. It was the correct solution. It was then sabotaged.</p>
      <p>The split between groups appears random. It is not. Lilly has separated natural leaders from established followers, placed caretakers away from the people they've bonded with, and distributed analytical players to ensure that both groups have enough competence to attempt the interleave solution with full confidence — and both groups have enough relational stakes to feel the weight of what failing it costs.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>Load tolerance engineering:</strong> Suspension bridges distribute weight across the entire cable system — a properly engineered bridge of this length can tolerate hundreds of tonnes of distributed load with minimal oscillation. The sabotage Lilly performed was not dramatic. It was precise: she reduced the cable tension calibration at the midpoint anchors by approximately 18%. This reduction is below the threshold that any visual inspection would detect, and below the threshold that individual load creates a perceptible response. Only the combined midpoint load of two complete groups triggers it.</p>
      <p>When the two groups meet at the bridge's centre, the combined weight creates a resonance frequency in the cable system that matches the natural oscillation period of the bridge structure. Rather than damping (as a properly tensioned bridge would), this creates <strong>Tacoma-Narrows-style positive feedback oscillation</strong> — each oscillation adds energy to the next, increasing amplitude progressively. Within 90 seconds of both groups reaching the midpoint, the oscillation is sufficient to prevent forward movement. The bridge is not collapsing. It is rhythmically swaying with increasing amplitude, and stopping is the only way to interrupt the feedback loop.</p>
      <p>Players who attempt to push through — who try to move forward during oscillation — will find that their forward momentum is working against an alternating lateral force. They will not fall. But they will not progress. And every second of attempted progress removes seconds from the wind clock.</p>
      <p><strong>What the analytical players calculated:</strong> The interleave solution is mathematically sound for the original bridge specifications. Players with structural engineering or physics base life skills will arrive at it correctly, with confidence, having done the actual calculation. They will not know the specifications have been changed because there is no way to know. The sabotage was performed before they arrived. Their solution is right. It just isn't right for this bridge.</p>
      <p><strong>The crisis decision neuroscience:</strong> When the oscillation lock hits, the group faces a genuine emergency requiring immediate coordinated action. Research on group decision-making under time pressure consistently shows that groups default to one of two failure modes: paralysed democracy (everyone speaks, nobody decides, consensus process consumes the available time) or false consensus (the loudest voice prevails without genuine buy-in, generating covert resistance that slows execution). The only path to fast, unified action is <strong>pre-established trust in a single decision-maker</strong> — what the literature calls "coordinating authority." Groups that produced that authority in games 1 and 2 make the call in under 60 seconds. Groups that didn't spend 8–12 minutes discussing and miss the wind window.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">The Righteousness Test</div>
      <p>The Clubs suit designation for this game is not cosmetic. Righteousness — 義 — in classical Chinese philosophical tradition is not noble self-sacrifice. It is doing what is <em>correct</em> even when it costs you something real, without requiring the action to feel heroic. The group that goes back loses time. The group that flattens against the guide ropes loses autonomy and must trust that the group rushing through won't knock them off the bridge. Neither action is heroic. Both are simply correct.</p>
      <p>The game doesn't eliminate for selfishness. It eliminates for the specific failure mode of being unable to produce a trusted single voice in a crisis — the failure of a group to crystallise into something that can act. The players who die on the bridge are not the bad people. They are the people whose social system, formed under extreme pressure over 48 hours, never produced the architecture that allows 30 people to move as one on a 60-second decision.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="harvest-box">
      <p>The Clubs round produces what Lilly calls the cleanest harvest in the early cycle: the life-force output of people making sacrifices for strangers they've known for less than 48 hours. The emotional quality of this output is distinct from fear (which is high-amplitude but thin) or cooperation (which is steady but moderate). It has the specific signature of <em>chosen cost</em> — the person who flattens against the rope has decided that the person rushing through is worth the risk of being knocked off. That decision, made in under 10 seconds with incomplete information, generates a life-force spike that Lilly uses as a calibration baseline for the rest of the cycle.</p>
