      <p><strong>The inverse vitality mechanic — Life domain biology:</strong> The lantern's brightness is directly proportional to the player's current life-force output. This is not metaphorical. The Life domain works on biological gradients — the movement of life-force from regions of high concentration to regions of low concentration, following the same principles as chemical diffusion, osmotic pressure, and electrochemical gradients in human physiology.</p>
      <p>A player in high physiological arousal — elevated sympathetic tone, high heart rate, elevated cortisol and adrenaline, active forward-planning cognition — is outputting life-force at a high rate. Their lantern is bright. β017's ambient biological substrate — the unstructured life-force of a dead universe without a warden — is not intelligent, but it is responsive to the same gradient principles. It moves toward higher concentrations of life-force the way a chemical moves down a concentration gradient. The bright lantern is a beacon to something that drains.</p>
      <p><strong>The clinical correlate:</strong> What this mechanic punishes is precisely what adaptive stress responses produce. Healthy response to threat: elevated arousal, rapid cognition, goal-directed behaviour. All three increase lantern brightness. All three increase targeting. The game has inverted the survival advantage of a healthy stress response into a survival liability.</p>
      <p><strong>The hope spike:</strong> The highest life-force output moment in the entire game occurs when a player sees the exit and experiences the relief-anticipation compound emotion — the neurochemical mix of dopamine (reward anticipation), serotonin (safety signal), and noradrenaline (approach motivation). This compound state produces the single brightest individual light moment in the district. It occurs at the exact moment the player is physically closest to winning. The drain pressure is at maximum. The exit is right there. The player who ran well and is seeing the exit for the first time is in the most danger they've been in all night.</p>
