    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Group Size Problem</div>
      <p>Players who have been travelling together since the Rat game's bond formation will instinctively want to stay together. Groups of four or five have been effective social units. In the Rabbit district, groups of four or five are a light cluster — a bright aggregate target that attracts drain from multiple directions simultaneously. The optimal travel group is two people. Two dimmed lanterns can cover each other's navigation blind spots without creating a combined signature that outweighs the benefit.</p>
      <p>Breaking the social groups formed over three games is a genuine psychological cost. The player who suggests splitting is asking their allies to trust that separation is safety. After forty-eight hours of learning that groups survive and individuals struggle, this is cognitively and emotionally counterintuitive. The players who insist on staying together because the group kept them safe so far will be the brightest things moving in the district.</p>
