    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>What the document contains:</strong> A complete reconstruction of one other player's base life — pre-wipe. Compiled from the wipe process data combined with Tiger Lily's observation system across games 1 through 4. The document is accurate, specific, and intimate: occupation and skill set, dominant decision patterns under pressure, relationship formation style, what the subject optimised for in each previous game, physical tells that emerged under stress, and one final detail that has no strategic value but is simply true about this person as a human being.</p>
      <p>That last element — the non-strategic true thing — is Tiger Lily's original design. She believed that a game about finding someone should require actually finding them, not just pattern-matching a strategic profile. Lilly kept it because the emotional response it generates when a player recognises someone from that specific detail — the moment of <em>I know exactly who this is</em> — produces the highest individual life-force spike in the entire 12-game cycle.</p>
      <p><strong>The neuroscience of recognition:</strong> The moment of correctly identifying someone from a behavioural profile rather than direct testimony activates what cognitive neuroscientists call the "aha" experience — a characteristic high-amplitude theta-wave burst in the anterior cingulate cortex combined with dopamine release in the nucleus accumbens. It is the same neurological signature as solving a complex puzzle, completing a creative work, or making a correct medical diagnosis from a differential. It feels like finding something that was always there.</p>
      <p>Now apply this to what the player has been asked to find: not a puzzle, but a <em>person</em>. The recognition event — finding the human being in the room who matches the document, confirmed by the non-strategic detail — produces an emotionally amplified version of this signature because it involves recognition of personhood, not just pattern completion. This is why Lilly specifically preserves the non-strategic true detail. Solving a strategic profile is interesting. Recognising a person is life-force output at maximum individual amplitude.</p>
      <p><strong>The elimination condition:</strong> Players submit a name at hour 12. Players who correctly identified their subject survive. Players who were not identified by anyone are eliminated — sent to the garden. Players who were identified survive regardless of whether they identified anyone else.</p>
      <p>The elimination condition for non-identification is neurologically brutal in its implications. The wipe removed autobiographical memory. What remained were base life skills, decision patterns, physical habits, relationship formation instincts — the deep structure of a person beneath their narrative self. The Dragon game asks: is that deep structure coherent enough to be legible to someone who is looking carefully?</p>
      <p>The players who are not identified are the players whose identity was primarily <strong>contextual</strong> — whose sense of self depended on the stories they told about themselves, the relationships that reflected them back, the accumulated autobiography that gave their behaviour meaning. When the wipe took the context, what remained wasn't enough for someone to find them by. They are not bad people. They are people for whom identity was largely social construction, and the wipe removed the construction's scaffolding. They cannot be found because the wipe took the most legible parts of them.</p>
