      <p>Every player is simultaneously trying to identify their document's subject, determine who holds their own document, prevent being identified by whoever holds it, and appear cooperative enough to avoid suspicion while intelligence-gathering. This requires maintaining four concurrent strategic frames while conducting genuine human interaction — a cognitive load that is, practically speaking, what experienced clinical interviewers, undercover investigators, and seasoned therapists manage professionally. Without their autobiographical memories, players have the base life skills for this work but not the conscious understanding of why they're good at it or how to narrate it to themselves.</p>
      <p>They're doing it on instinct. And the instinct is accurate.</p>
