    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Setup</div>
      <p>A Chinese dynastic consultation hall. Players seated in a horseshoe facing Tiger Lily on a raised platform — silent, watching, ledger open. Every player receives a card face-down containing a single hidden rule that applies only to them. No two rules are identical. No player knows anyone else's rule. The stated instruction: <em>"There are rules. Follow yours."</em></p>
      <p>Three violations: public revelation of your rule to the group. Six violations: elimination. Tiger Lily tracks violations silently but visibly — players can see she's marking but not who or how many times. The game ends not on a timer but when all players have identified all other players' rules.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>Individual rule examples and their interaction effects:</strong> Consider a player whose rule is "you cannot use the word 'I'" interacting with a player whose rule is "you must repeat the last word of any question directed at you before answering." A question directed at the second player will end with a word. They repeat it. The first player, who cannot say 'I', must respond to the exchange without first-person singular. These interactions compound as rules multiply — the consultation hall becomes a space where every utterance must be simultaneously run through the speaker's own constraint, monitored for its effect on others' constraints, and parsed for what it reveals about everyone else's constraints.</p>
      <p>This is functionally equivalent to what cognitive psychologists call the <strong>dual-task interference paradigm</strong>: performing two cognitively demanding tasks simultaneously degrades performance on both below the level of performing either alone. The Snake game forces players to perform four simultaneous cognitive tasks (follow own rule, detect own violations, monitor others for their rules, manage social interaction) under conditions of social observation where visible failure has consequences. The cognitive load is extraordinary.</p>
      <p><strong>The Nash equilibrium problem:</strong> Game theory describes a Nash equilibrium as a state where no individual player can improve their outcome by changing their strategy, given what everyone else is doing. In the Snake game, the Nash equilibrium is mutual defection: everyone observes and deduces without sharing, because sharing is a unilateral vulnerability in an environment where information has been weaponised for five games.</p>
      <p>But the Nash equilibrium is <strong>Pareto inferior</strong> — everyone is worse off under mutual defection than under mutual cooperation. If everyone shared their rules immediately, the game would end in two hours. Under mutual defection, it takes eight to ten hours and generates significant violation-debt and elimination. The game has constructed a Prisoner's Dilemma at scale: the collectively optimal strategy requires the one thing five games of competitive survival have trained players to never do — voluntary, unilateral disclosure of private information.</p>
      <p>The players who successfully override this trained response are not the ones who don't understand the danger of disclosure. They're the ones who understand it and do it anyway, because they have developed enough trust in the group's rationality to believe that disclosure will be reciprocated. This requires a theory-of-mind depth that goes beyond "can I model this person's behaviour" to "can I trust that this person is capable of the same override I'm performing right now." That is a profoundly difficult cognitive and social judgment to make after five games under survival pressure.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">What the Game Selects For</div>
      <p>The Snake game identifies players who can override survival instinct in favour of collective optimisation even when optimisation feels like exposure. This is not heroism. It is a specific cognitive-emotional capacity — the ability to hold genuine uncertainty about whether cooperation will be reciprocated, acknowledge that uncertainty explicitly, and act cooperatively anyway because the expected value calculation favours it even when the risk is real.</p>
      <p>Clinically, this maps closely to what attachment theorists call <strong>secure attachment</strong> under stress: the ability to trust others with information about yourself when trust has costs, because your internal working model of relationships says that other people are generally trustworthy enough for the risk to be worth taking. Players who were formed in environments where trust was consistently punished — and β017's culture of domination-as-love produces many such players — will not be able to perform this disclosure even when they intellectually understand it's optimal.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="harvest-box">
      <p>The Snake game doesn't produce spikes. It produces a long, sustained drain — the metabolic and psychological cost of sustained vigilance, continuous self-monitoring, and maintained social performance over eight to ten hours. Lilly uses it specifically to deplete players before the second half of the cycle. The games from 7 onward require emotional and cognitive resources that the Snake game systematically depletes. Players entering game 7 are tired in a way they can't quite locate — not physically exhausted, not clearly afraid, just emptied out from the sustained cost of being continuously watched while continuously watching.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lilly-interlude">
      The fastest path to winning is to say your rule out loud at the start. Everyone knows this by minute four. Not one person will do it for at least two hours, and usually not until someone has been publicly revealed by violation accumulation and the group sees what forced disclosure looks like and decides that voluntary disclosure is better than that. Even then, it will be one person, hesitantly, watching the room's reaction before anyone follows. The game runs on the human inability to be the first one to trust. I find this endlessly instructive.
    </div>

    <div class="mystery-closer">
      "The fastest way to win is to say your rule immediately. Walk in. Open your mouth. Say it. Done. Two hours. Everyone survives. I know this. I knew this at minute four. I am now in hour seven. I have not said it. Nobody has said it. The game will take nine hours because none of us can make ourselves do the obviously correct thing and the game built this room knowing that, and we are going to be unable to do it on schedule, and I think I might be the only one who realises that the game already won the moment it got us to sit down."
    </div>
  </section>


