    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>The psychology of false exits:</strong> Hope is not a diffuse emotional state. It is a specific cognitive process — what psychologists call <strong>agency thinking</strong> (belief in one's capacity to find pathways) combined with <strong>pathway thinking</strong> (identification of specific routes to a goal). Both components are necessary for functional hope. The Horse game targets the pathway thinking component specifically.</p>
      <p>Players who walk through the gate are not naïve. They are not stupid. They are players who have maintained genuine hope across six games — who helped others in the Rabbit game at cost to themselves, who disclosed in the Snake game despite the risk, who chose someone in the Tiger game and trusted them. They are, by any measure, the players who have demonstrated the most prosocial, cooperative, genuinely moral behaviour in the cycle.</p>
      <p>The gate is open. After six games of invisible walls and hidden rules and modified bridges, the gate is simply open. For a player who has maintained hope, this is evidence that the exit is real. The hope hypothesis is confirmed. They walk through.</p>
      <p><strong>What happens in the unstructured remainder:</strong> The drain is not immediate. Players who enter feel, for approximately the first thirty minutes, that they're finally free. The city is receding behind them. The horizon is ahead. Then the distance doesn't change. Twenty minutes more and the horizon is still the same distance. Players with spatial reasoning skills begin calculating — they're covering ground, their velocity is measurable, the horizon should be getting closer. It isn't. The geometry is wrong.</p>
      <p>Then the gates they came through are no longer visible. The city is gone. There's nothing behind them but the unstructured remainder in all directions. The drain has been operating since they crossed the threshold; at the ninety-minute mark, the physical symptoms of life-force depletion begin — fatigue, loss of peripheral sensation, cognitive slowing. The player's lantern from game 4 would be very bright right now, in the recognition-horror of understanding what is happening.</p>
      <p>They have approximately ninety more minutes. The hope doesn't die quickly. That is the specific cruelty the Spades suit designates. The hope that kept them moral across six games — the hope that was their greatest asset — now keeps them walking toward a horizon that will not arrive until the drain completes.</p>
