  <section class="game-section">
    <div class="game-header">
      <div class="game-animal">🐑</div>
      <div class="game-meta">
        <p class="game-number">Game 08 · The Chinese Zodiac Sequence</p>
        <h2 class="game-title">GOAT — The Ward</h2>
        <p class="game-subtitle">Medical Resource Allocation / Impossible Triage / Moral Injury Harvest</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Setup</div>
      <p>A hospital ward. Anachronistic for β017's period aesthetic — Tiger Lily built it because care required it. Dutch structural bones, Chinese medicine philosophy. Functionally real medical equipment that exists because Tiger Lily's Life domain made it possible in a dead universe. Players are divided on arrival into CAREGIVERS and PATIENTS at a 1:4 ratio. Patients have visible condition indicators — legible to players with medical base life skills. All conditions are real, treatable with available resources, and untreatable with available resources if treated simultaneously. The ward has enough supplies to treat 60% of patients to full recovery and stabilise the rest. Eight hours. Patients receiving neither treatment nor stabilisation go to the garden. Caregivers whose ward falls below 60% survival fall with their patients.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>Triage logic vs. emotional allocation:</strong> Standard triage protocol — START triage or similar systems — prioritises patients by treatability, not acuity. The principle is resource efficiency: treat patients who will survive with minimal intervention first; reserve complex resource use for patients who cannot survive without it but will survive with it; identify patients who will not survive regardless and use minimal resources to maintain comfort. This protocol produces the highest total number of survivors with limited resources.</p>
      <p>In the Goat game's resource arithmetic, this means treating the most stable patients first. A stable patient who can be treated to full recovery with two units of supplies produces a survivor. A critical patient who requires twelve units and may or may not survive produces uncertainty at six times the resource cost. Under the 60% threshold constraint, the mathematically optimal strategy is explicit: begin with the cheapest recoveries, bank efficiency, and use remaining resources on critical cases whose treatment cost falls within the remaining budget.</p>
      <p>This strategy requires walking past a patient in critical condition to treat a patient who appears fine. Every instinct in a caretaking-profiled human being rebels against this. The instinct is not irrational — in an environment with unlimited resources, treating critical patients first is correct. But the instinct does not update for resource constraints. It responds to visible suffering, not to resource arithmetic.</p>
      <p><strong>The hidden variable — the begging patient:</strong> Among the patient population is an individual who will request treatment verbally and persistently. This patient's condition places them in the middle tier — treatable but not a resource-efficiency priority. Their verbal appeal bypasses the analytical allocation process and activates the emotional allocation process directly. Caregivers who allocate by emotional response will spend resources on this patient early, reducing availability for the high-efficiency stable patients, and ultimately fall below threshold.</p>
      <p>This is not a flaw in the caregiver's character. It is a documented response pattern in clinical settings. <strong>The identifiable victim effect</strong> — the tendency to respond more strongly to a specific, named, visible individual in distress than to statistical lives that could be saved — is a robust finding in behavioural economics and clinical ethics. It does not indicate bad medicine. It indicates normal human empathy interacting with a resource constraint the empathy system was not designed to navigate. The game is not testing whether caregivers are good people. It is testing whether they can override a deeply adaptive human response under extreme time pressure with insufficient information.</p>
      <p><strong>The second hidden variable — deliberate patient allocation:</strong> Patients include players identified in the Dragon game as strategically dangerous: high analytical profiles, leadership emergence profiles, the players Lilly wants weakened or removed before the final four games. The caretaking-profiled players most likely to be assigned the caregiver role are also the players most likely to allocate by emotional pressure. The arrangement ensures that the players best positioned to survive the final games are in the care of the players most likely to make emotionally-driven triage decisions. If those caregivers fail threshold, both groups go to the garden together.</p>
      <p><strong>Moral injury:</strong> The caregivers who succeed — who walk past the begging patient and execute the resource-efficient allocation correctly and meet the 60% threshold — carry a specific psychological wound out of the Goat game. <strong>Moral injury</strong> is distinguished from PTSD in clinical literature by its cause: not the experience of something done to you, but the experience of having done something correct that violated your moral framework. The caregiver who made the right decision and saved the ward knows they made the right decision. They also cannot stop thinking about the patient they walked past. Both things are true simultaneously and they don't resolve each other.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="harvest-box">
      <p>The Goat game's harvest is unique in the cycle: it collects moral injury, which Lilly values because it <em>persists</em>. The players who survive game 8 carry the ward with them into games 9 through 12. Every subsequent moment of decision-making is shadowed by the memory of having made a correct decision that felt terrible. The moral injury doesn't diminish decision-making capacity — it complicates it. It adds a second evaluation layer to every choice: not just "what is the correct action" but "what will it cost me to live with having done it." This added cognitive and emotional load is Lilly's long-term harvest from the Goat game. She doesn't extract it at the end of game 8. She extracts it across the remaining games as it accumulates.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lilly-interlude">
      The Goat game is the kindest thing Tiger Lily ever designed. She built a functioning hospital in a dead universe because she thought the players deserved to experience care — to be cared for and to care for others — even in a game about survival. She staffed it with real conditions and real medicine. The ward works. The treatments work. She just didn't build it with enough resources for everyone, because she wanted the caregivers to face the actual problem of care under scarcity. She thought the problem was beautiful. I kept the ward exactly as she designed it. I only added the begging patient. One small addition to a beautiful design. It was enough.
    </div>

    <div class="mystery-closer">
      "The correct answer is to ignore the person begging you for help and treat the person who looks fine. I know that's correct. The triage math is not complicated. I can see the resource budget. I can see the patient conditions. I know what the protocol says. I would walk to the begging patient. I would sit with them. I would use the wrong resources. I would fail the threshold. I would go to the garden for the correct human reason and the game would count it as the wrong one and I think — I genuinely think — that might be the most honest thing any of these twelve games says about who we actually are."
    </div>
  </section>


