    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Setup</div>
      <p>The VOC administrative archive — rows of filing systems, document shelves, the preserved bureaucratic infrastructure of a dead civilisation. Documents arrive via a mechanical delivery system: one document every 45 seconds per player. Twelve filing categories. File correctly. At 50 errors: garden. At intervals of 30, 60, 90, and 120 minutes, subsets of categories shift definitions without announcement. At the 121-minute mark, the system resets to original definitions — but resets from the shifted state, meaning players who correctly adapted to the shifts must now re-adapt to the original system they've overwritten.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>Working memory and rule-set management:</strong> The human working memory system — prefrontal cortex and dorsolateral networks — maintains approximately four independent "chunks" of information simultaneously under optimal conditions. Under cognitive load, this capacity decreases. Under stress, it decreases further. The Monkey game demands simultaneous maintenance of: current rule set, historical rule set for cross-referencing, ongoing pattern-detection for the next shift, peripheral awareness of other players' error markers, and motor execution of filing at 45-second intervals without allowing monitoring to interrupt physical task performance.</p>
      <p>This is five cognitive demands. Working memory has capacity for four under optimal conditions. Players are not in optimal conditions — they are eight games into a survival scenario with compounding psychological load from the moral injury of game 8, the depletion of the Snake game, and the accumulated social weight of having watched people they care about go to the garden.</p>
      <p><strong>The 121-minute reset attack:</strong> This is the Monkey game's most refined cruelty. Players who detected the shifts and adapted correctly — who updated their mental model of the filing system at each shift — have now overwritten their original rule-set representation. The reset at minute 121 returns to original definitions, but the players' working memory contains the shifted version, not the original. They must now re-learn a system they already knew, while under error accumulation pressure, with the cognitive overhead of recognising that they're back to something they once understood but can no longer access cleanly.</p>
      <p>This is clinically analogous to the experience in cognitive rehabilitation of patients who have learned a compensatory strategy for a deficit and then have the deficit partially resolve: they are now working with two competing motor programs or memory systems, and the original system interferes with the compensatory one in ways that feel worse than the original deficit. The game is creating a iatrogenic — self-caused — cognitive impairment by rewarding adaptation and then resetting the environment the adaptation was built for.</p>
      <p><strong>The document content:</strong> The archive contains β017's actual history. Without their memories, players don't know what they're touching. But base life skills include reading comprehension, document interpretation, and the ability to understand what a record means even without its social context. Some players begin reading what they're filing. They slow to 80-second intervals. Their error count climbs. But they are holding the records of a dead civilisation — birth records, trade agreements, judicial proceedings — and something in the base life responds to it.</p>
      <p>Tiger Lily placed one genuine document into the delivery system: her own file, entering at hour 3. A complete record of who Tiger Lily was before Lilly's directives reduced her to four words. It doesn't fit any filing category. It never will. Whatever player receives it faces a choice: force it into the closest available category (incurring an error) or hold it while the next documents pile up (incurring more errors). There is no correct response to Tiger Lily's file under the Monkey game's rules. The game's rules don't have a category for the personhood of someone whose personhood has been overwritten.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="harvest-box">
      <p>The Monkey game drains cognitive reserves systematically in preparation for games 10 through 12. Lilly needs players depleted but functional — not broken, not in crisis, just operating at reduced capacity. The specific harvest is the life-force output of a mind at maximum sustained capacity against a moving target, which produces a steady, medium-amplitude output over three to four hours. Combined with the Snake game's vigilance drain and the Goat game's moral injury accumulation, players entering the final three games are carrying a weight they can't fully locate or name. They're tired in ways that rest won't fix. This is by design.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lilly-interlude">
      Tiger Lily's file enters the archive at hour three. I kept this. I kept every element of the archive exactly as she designed it — the real history, the real records, the real care she put into representing a dead civilisation's administrative texture. I kept it all. I only added the shift mechanic. And then I set the shift intervals so that by the time Tiger Lily's file arrives, the player who receives it is at approximately 28 errors and has just re-adapted from the 90-minute shift. They get her file at the worst possible moment. They cannot correctly file it. They cannot put it down. They are holding proof that the warden who designed this place to be beautiful was real, and they are about to be eliminated for not knowing where to put her.
    </div>

    <div class="mystery-closer">
      "The rules changed. I adapted. The rules changed again. I adapted to the adaptation. The rules reset to the original, which I have overwritten. I am now operating with two competing filing systems and 31 errors and the document in my hands appears to be a complete record of a person who designed these games to be survivable, and I cannot find the category for 'evidence that something was made with love before it was made into a weapon,' and I have 45 seconds before the next document arrives, and I don't know which of these feelings is the one that's going to get me eliminated."
    </div>
  </section>


