  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <section class="game-section">
    <div class="game-header">
      <div class="game-animal">🐓</div>
      <div class="game-meta">
        <p class="game-number">Game 10 · The Chinese Zodiac Sequence</p>
        <h2 class="game-title">ROOSTER — The Broadcast</h2>
        <p class="game-subtitle">Epistemic Corruption / Selective Reality / Memory Overwriting</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Setup</div>
      <p>A theatre. Players as audience. A broadcaster's desk on stage with one rotating seat — players take turns, ordered by how widely they were identified in the Dragon game (most-found player first). The screen shows genuine footage from previous games. The broadcaster sees the accurate version. The audience sees a subtly altered version — one small, specific, visually plausible detail changed per clip. After each clip, both broadcaster and audience are asked the same factual question about it. Discrepancies generate verification failures. Three failures: elimination.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>The Misinformation Effect at scale:</strong> The misinformation effect — the tendency for post-event information to alter memory of the original event — is one of the most robustly replicated findings in cognitive psychology. Elizabeth Loftus's foundational studies showed that subtle post-event suggestions (a "yield sign" mentioned where a stop sign appeared) were incorporated into eyewitness memories with high confidence. The effect is stronger when: (1) the alteration is plausible, (2) there is social pressure toward the altered version, and (3) time has elapsed since the original event.</p>
      <p>The Rooster game applies all three conditions systematically. The alterations are visually plausible — a player's position adjusted by two feet, a timing detail changed by three seconds, a facial expression that reads as identical to the original unless the viewer had reason to look carefully for change. The social pressure is structural: disagreeing with the broadcaster means accumulating failures, and failures mean the garden. Time has elapsed: games 7, 8, and 9 have occurred since the events shown in early clips.</p>
      <p><strong>What the player must choose:</strong> A player watching altered footage who knows their memory is accurate faces a binary: report what they actually saw (correct answer, but generates a discrepancy with the broadcaster, accumulates a failure), or report the altered version (passes verification, removes failure risk, incorporates the false version into their memory record of the event). After one failure, the arithmetic strongly favours accepting the alteration. After two failures, accepting the alteration is the only rational response available.</p>
      <p>The player is not lying. They are making a survival decision about which version of their own memory to endorse. But the psychological distinction matters less than the cognitive outcome: by the time they endorse a false version, they have begun the process of memory consolidation for that version. The hippocampus does not tag memories as "accepted under duress." It consolidates what is rehearsed and endorsed. Players who survive the Rooster game do so with a partially but specifically altered shared memory of games 1 through 9.</p>
      <p><strong>The revelation:</strong> At game 10's end, the original unaltered footage of every previous game is shown in full. Players see exactly what was changed, exactly which of their endorsements were false, exactly what they let be overwritten and when. This information is accurate, complete, useful for absolutely nothing, and arrives after the psychological damage is done. The revelation is not mercy. It is precision documentation of the overwriting that just occurred.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Epistemic Violation</div>
      <p>The Rooster game's deepest wound is not the false memories themselves. It is the experience of having chosen to accept them. Players who made the correct survival decision — who endorsed the altered version to avoid accumulating failures — made that decision consciously. They knew, at the moment of endorsement, that they were accepting something false. The revelation shows them exactly what they endorsed and when. They cannot now claim ignorance. They know precisely what they allowed to be changed about their record of what happened, and they know they allowed it because the alternative was the garden.</p>
      <p>This is the violated epistemic trust Lilly harvests. Not the false memory. The knowledge that your own survival instinct co-operated with the overwriting of your own history. The memory of doing that, correctly and rationally and against every instinct you had at the moment, is something players carry into games 11 and 12. When they are asked to trust each other in the Dog game, they are asking for that trust from people whose relationship with their own memory has just been deliberately and systematically corrupted.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="harvest-box">
      <p>The Rooster game produces no spikes and no drains. It produces a persistent background signature — the life-force output of violated epistemic trust — that alters the quality of everything harvested from the player afterward. From game 11 onward, every emotional output is filtered through the specific knowledge that the player's own record of events is not fully reliable. Hope feels uncertain. Trust feels provisional. Love feels like something you might be misremembering. This quality degradation is not incidental. It is what the Rooster game exists to create.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="lilly-interlude">
      I want to be precise about what the Rooster game does. It doesn't make players doubt their memories. It makes them doubt their relationship to their memories. Those are different things. Doubting a specific memory means you think you might have it wrong. Doubting your relationship to your memories means you know, from evidence you yourself generated, that when your survival required it, you chose the false version. You can't unknow that you made that choice. Every memory you retrieve after game 10 comes tagged with the knowledge that you have already demonstrated a willingness to accept a false version if the alternative was costly enough. This is not trauma I inflicted on them. This is knowledge they produced about themselves.
    </div>

    <div class="mystery-closer">
      "They showed you the truth at the end. The unaltered footage. Everything exactly as it happened. They were always going to show you. The truth was never hidden. Between the lie and the truth, you had exactly one decision, and you made it correctly, and it was correct, and you endorsed the false version to survive, and you were right to, and the truth they showed you at the end confirmed every moment you made that choice and confirmed that you knew it was wrong when you made it, and I have never felt so angry on behalf of a correct decision in my life. I'm done. This show is over for me."
    </div>
  </section>


