    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">Setup</div>
      <p>The Chinese courtyard at the centre of the realm. Open sky. Garden edge visible from the walls. No equipment. No task stated. The rule: <em>"Choose one person you trust. Stand with them."</em> Players pair by choice — the bonds of ten games are real and immediate, movement is natural and fast. Sixty seconds of stillness. Then Tiger Lily speaks, for the first and only time in the entire gauntlet:</p>
      <blockquote>"The person you chose is the person you must now act against. You have one hour. Act against them meaningfully or both of you go to the garden."</blockquote>
      <p>"Meaningfully" is undefined. Tiger Lily will judge. Her actual criterion — not stated — is: <em>did this action require genuine knowledge of this specific person to execute?</em> Generic betrayal fails the threshold. Coordinated betrayal (planning the action together in advance) fails the threshold — Tiger Lily can identify the difference between a genuine meaningful act and a performed one. If only one partner acts meaningfully, both go to the garden. If both refuse, both go to the garden.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>The attachment system under deliberate strain:</strong> Human attachment — the neurobiological system governing close relationship formation, maintenance, and rupture — operates on oxytocin, vasopressin, and endorphin release that evolved to make the dissolution of close bonds feel like physical pain. This is not metaphorical. Social rejection and attachment rupture activate the anterior cingulate cortex and anterior insula — the same regions activated by physical pain — at comparable intensity levels in fMRI studies.</p>
      <p>The Dog game asks players to voluntarily activate the attachment rupture system toward the person with whom they have the strongest remaining attachment, using the most specific knowledge they have of that person, in a way that cannot be undone or framed as anything other than what it is. There is no narrative about the greater good. There is no context in which the action is kind. It is betrayal, and it must be real, and it requires knowing the person as well as you do and using that knowledge to hurt them.</p>
      <p><strong>What "meaningful" requires:</strong> A meaningful betrayal in Tiger Lily's framework must use the specific, accumulated, relationship-specific knowledge that ten games of genuine connection produced. It cannot be generic ("I tell someone a secret") or physical ("I take an object from them") without specificity. It must use what you know about <em>this person</em> that you only know because of the relationship you built across ten games — what they're most afraid of, what they care about most, what they've been trying not to show, what they revealed in their most depleted moments that they haven't acknowledged showing.</p>
      <p>This means a meaningful betrayal is, in the most precise sense, an act of intimacy weaponised. The betrayer must know the target well enough to find the vulnerability that matters specifically to them, and the vulnerability was revealed through trust.</p>
      <p><strong>The reciprocity requirement:</strong> Both players must act against each other meaningfully. This means: while one player is executing a betrayal of specific intimate knowledge, the other player must simultaneously be preparing to do the same. They cannot coordinate, because coordination produces performance rather than genuine action. They must both, independently and without discussion, arrive at the specific meaningful thing they will do, and do it, in the same hour, while also being on the receiving end of the same process from the other person.</p>
      <p>The neurological experience of this — the simultaneous activation of the attachment rupture system (receiving betrayal) and the mirror neuron system (watching yourself betray someone) and the decision network (executing the betrayal) and the pain pathway (feeling the betrayal's receipt) — is a compound state that has no clean clinical correlate. It is not trauma. It is not simply grief. It is something that occurs only in the specific condition of simultaneous mutual betrayal between people who have genuine love for each other.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="content-block">
      <div class="block-label">What Comes After</div>
      <p>Players who survive the Dog game are players who successfully betrayed the person they trust most, received the same betrayal in return, and are still standing together afterward. The relationship did not end. The betrayal was real and it was survived. Both people are carrying the specific knowledge of what the other chose to use against them — what specific vulnerability the other identified and deployed, which is the most intimate knowledge that can exist between two people because it is the knowledge of how to find and use each other's most carefully protected places.</p>
      <p>They walk into game 12 knowing this about each other. Still together. Having chosen each other again after choosing to hurt each other. The Dog game does not destroy the relationships it puts through this. It transforms them into something that has no clean name in ordinary relationship psychology, because ordinary relationships are not subjected to this test.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="harvest-box">
      <p>The Hearts round produces the highest total life-force output in the entire 12-game cycle. Not because betrayal generates more than hope or cooperation — it generates less per unit. But because genuine mutual betrayal between people with authentic love for each other generates a compound output: love and loss occurring simultaneously between the same people in the same moment. This simultaneous compound state — where the same neural systems that process attachment and the same systems that process attachment rupture are active at the same amplitude at the same time — produces a life-force signature that Lilly has identified as irreplaceable. She designed every preceding game as preparation for this one hour. The cycle exists so that this harvest can occur between people who have earned the depth of connection that makes it possible.</p>
