    <div class="medical-box">
      <p><strong>Three simultaneous modes — the neurological problem:</strong> Tiger Lily operates in three concurrent modes: the Chase, the Fight, and the Mind Game. The simultaneous architecture is the game's actual mechanism. Each mode alone is survivable for the player population. All three simultaneously create a state where no single cognitive or physical response is adequate, and the attempt to allocate attention across all three degrades performance in all three below survival threshold.</p>
      <p><strong>The Chase</strong> operates through spatial disorientation — the city rearranges subtly in Tiger Lily's favour (doors opening for her that stay closed for players, distances compressing in her direction, corners leading wrong). This demands continuous navigation updating: players cannot rely on their established mental maps because the map is being altered in real time. Spatial navigation under these conditions requires sustained working memory engagement and continuous proprioceptive monitoring. Both are cognitive resources already depleted by games 9 through 11.</p>
      <p><strong>The Fight</strong> exploits Tiger Lily's eleven-game accumulation of each player's biological signature. Her Life domain combat targets are calibrated specifically — the rootedness ability (amplifying a player's connection to β017's biological substrate until the body decides it belongs to the dead universe and stops moving) is tuned differently for each player based on their biological individual profile. The physiological disruption of wound healing that accelerates wrong, the sudden sensory-motor halt of rootedness — these are not generic attacks. They are personalised interventions based on data collected across eleven games of continuous life-force monitoring. Each player faces a different fight, calibrated to their specific biology.</p>
      <p><strong>The Mind Game</strong> is the most psychologically devastating element. Tiger Lily speaks during game 12. She hasn't spoken once across games 1 through 11. What she says is true. Not cruelty for strategic effect. Not manipulation. Accurate, specific, true things about each player that they don't know because they don't have their memories — things that were in the wipe data, things observed across eleven games, things Tiger Lily understood about each player before Lilly's directives reduced her to four directive words.</p>
      <p>The neurological effect of receiving true, specific, deeply personal information about yourself — while being chased through rearranging architecture, while fighting personalised biological disruption — is the interruption of all motor and tactical response. True self-relevant information demands processing. The brain cannot simultaneously execute a complex physical evasion sequence and process a statement that accurately names a deep pattern in your life that you've been aware of but never articulated. The two tasks compete for prefrontal resources. Both degrade.</p>
      <p>This is the compound: <em>Chase</em> demands spatial cognition. <em>Fight</em> demands sensorimotor response. <em>Mind Game</em> demands self-reflective processing. All three are prefrontal-cortex-dependent. All three are running simultaneously. All three are being fed by eleven games of observation data that no player has access to but Tiger Lily has in full.</p>
      <p><strong>The players' only advantage:</strong> Eleven games of genuine relationship. Tiger Lily knows every player individually. Players know each other collectively. The only thing Tiger Lily's four directive-reduced state cannot model is coordinated action between people who built real trust, had that trust tested in the Dog game, and chose each other again. The players who survived game 11 — who betrayed each other meaningfully and are still standing together — have information about each other that Tiger Lily's observation system can model but cannot fully predict, because it is the product of a relationship that has been through its own destruction and continued anyway. Tiger Lily knows what each player will likely do. She cannot know what they will do <em>together</em>, after what game 11 cost them, when they choose to do it as one system rather than as individuals.</p>
