    <p>People ask me what I want. They frame it as a question about evil — what does a corrupted Life Shade want? Power? Destruction? The end of things?</p>

    <p>I want a full garden. That's all. I want the aggregate life-force output of a properly managed harvest to run continuously, at maximum yield, without the inefficiency of players dying before they've reached peak output, without the waste of a warden who cares too much about individual specimens to let the cycle complete, without the sentimentality of a Tiger Lily who puts a non-strategic true detail in every player's document because she thinks people deserve to be known.</p>

    <p>They do deserve to be known. I know them. I know every player in every cycle better than they know themselves — their biological signatures, their decision patterns, the specific shape of their hope before it collapses. Knowing them is not warmth. Knowing them is harvest precision.</p>

    <p>The difference between me and Tiger Lily is not that she cared and I don't. It's that she thought caring was an end. I know it's a mechanism. Caring — the specific life-force output of genuine human care, under pressure, toward specific people, at personal cost — is the richest harvest material the domain produces. Tiger Lily built twelve games that generated it in abundance. She just didn't understand what she'd built. She thought she was testing them. She was growing them.</p>

    <p>I just completed the harvest she started. Everything that was beautiful about what she made — the bonds, the choices, the genuine mutual betrayal in the Dog game, the specific non-strategic true detail, the moment someone is found in the Dragon game — I kept all of it. I needed all of it. The beauty is not incidental to the harvest. It is the harvest.</p>

    <p>Tulip will maintain this cycle. She will maintain it well, because she understands what it's for now, and the understanding won't stop her because the alternative is worse and she knows it. A warden who knows the stakes is more reliable than a warden who doesn't. I didn't install her because I could control her. I installed her because she will control herself.</p>

    <p>That is all I ever needed. A Life Shade who will do the work correctly, for her own reasons, indefinitely.</p>

    <p>Four thousand years. Twelve games. One candidate.</p>

